# AmiTCP_NG stack configuration (AmiTCP:db/AmiTCP.config).
# The loopback interface (127.0.0.1) is brought up automatically at startup and
# local traffic always uses it -- there is no longer a useloopback knob.

# This machine's own name -- what gethostname() reports to applications.
# Without it the name is an empty string, which some software handles badly:
# it is not an error, so a program gets "" back and carries on with it. Change
# it to whatever you like; if you already had this file, the installer leaves
# your setting alone.
HOSTNAME=amiga

# --- Logging -----------------------------------------------------------------
# The stack keeps a quiet log of anything that goes wrong, in a file, and shows
# you nothing. That is the default and most people never need to change it: if
# something breaks there is already a record, and if nothing breaks the file is
# a couple of lines per boot.
#
# Every build can do this. There is no special "debug build" to obtain and no
# version that lacks it, so if you are ever asked for a log, raise the detail
# level below to 7, reproduce the problem, and send the file it names.

# Master switch, default ON. OFF records nothing at all -- not even emergencies,
# so a panic would leave no trace -- and log calls are skipped at the point they
# are made, so nothing is formatted or written and next to no time is spent.
# The detail level below is remembered either way.
#LOGGING=OFF

# How much detail. These are the standard syslog priorities, and each level
# includes everything more serious than it:
#   0 = emergencies only    3 = errors        5 = notices (the default)
#   1 = alerts              4 = warnings      6 = information
#   2 = critical                              7 = debug
# LOGLEVEL=7 is what to set if you have been asked for a debug log; it also turns
# on the API tracer, which names any library call that fails and the errno it
# failed with -- that is what identifies a program the stack is not satisfying.
# Note 0 is "emergencies only", NOT off -- use LOGGING=OFF above for that.
#LOGLEVEL=7

# Also throw log messages at a console window, as well as the file. Default OFF,
# because that window puts itself in front of whatever you are doing -- which is
# useful while you are watching for something and a nuisance the rest of the
# time. It has no effect on the log FILE, which is written either way, and while
# it is off no window and no console handler are opened at all.
# CONSOLENAME= chooses which window; the default is a plain CON: on the Workbench
# screen.
#LOGCONSOLE=ON

# Where it is written. The default is RAM:AmiTCP.log, which needs no assign and
# is always available -- but it is gone at the next reboot, so if you are chasing
# something that only clears with a reboot, point this at a disk instead. If the
# file cannot be opened the stack falls back to T:, then AmiTCP:, then SYS:, and
# says which one it settled on (and why) in the first line of the log.
#LOGFILENAME=RAM:AmiTCP.log

# --- Diagnostics ---------------------------------------------------------------

# Check the packet-buffer (mbuf) pool for corruption. Default OFF.
#
# Turn this ON only when asked to, or when chasing a fault where the network
# gets gradually slower and then stops with nothing in the log. It makes the
# stack mark every buffer as it is released and check it when it is handed out
# again, which catches a buffer released twice, or written to after it was
# released -- faults that otherwise do their damage silently and only show up
# later as corrupted traffic or a stack that has stopped sending.
#
# It costs speed on every packet, which is why it is off by default. Findings
# are written to the log, so keep LOGGING on and LOGFILENAME pointed at a disk.
# This one is read only at startup: change it, then restart the stack.
#MBUFCHECK=ON

# --- Let the network card write straight into memory ---------------------------
#
# ON by default. This line only exists so you can turn it OFF.
#
# Normally, when a frame arrives, your network card's driver copies the bytes into
# the stack one call at a time. SANA-II allows a second way: the driver asks for an
# address and moves the data itself, with no copying at all. For a card that can do
# this, that removes an entire pass over every packet received -- real work on a
# 68k.
#
# It is on because it costs nothing to offer. A driver that cannot do it ignores
# the offer and carries on copying exactly as before, which is what every driver
# did before this existed. Most Amiga drivers are in that group.
#
# If your machine misbehaves with a card that does use it, put:
#
#   SANADMA=NO
#
# and the stack goes back to copying every frame. Please say so if you need to --
# a driver that DMAs is rare enough that we would want to know.
#SANADMA=NO

# --- Run-time options ----------------------------------------------------------
#
# Most of the stack's internal options are NOT set here. They are read and changed
# with the AmiTCPControl command:
#
#   AmiTCPControl                        list every option and its value
#   AmiTCPControl GET tcp.iw             show one
#   AmiTCPControl SAVE SET udp.cksum=0   change it and keep it across reboots
#
# Saved options live in ENVARC:AmiTCP_NG and are read when the stack starts.
# Anything set in THIS file wins over a saved option, wherever the line appears.
# Options marked "(auto)" are worked out from this machine's RAM, CPU and network
# speed and cannot be set -- those values are already tuned for your hardware.
